;Match details

* '''Match 1: Kuroki def. Ishida.''' Seigigun brother against Seigigun brother — the faction's internal honor match, fought clean from bell to bell. Ishida, rebounding from his emotional win over Diallo, controlled with the veteran's grapping; Kuroki, still searching for his first points, answered with the Black Iron strong style. The finish: Ishida went for the Ishida Lock at 13 minutes; Kuroki powered out, hit the Black Iron Bomb at 14:31. Kuroki's first points of the tournament — and the first blemish on Ishida's rebound. The two embraced after the match. '''Crowd:''' Respectful dueling chants — '''SEIGIGUN''' unified the building. APPLAUSE for the embrace.

* '''Match 2: Ono def. Reyes.''' The young lion's first points. Ono, 0-2 and reeling after the flash cradle that nearly stole the KAZUKI match, faced Reyes — the Cobra, the tag champion, winless and dangerous. Reyes's lucha rhythm had Ono off-balance for ten minutes; the Cobra Twist nearly ended it at 11. Ono's answer was pure Seigigun: the basement dropkick, the European clutch, and at 12:47 the Ono Driver — his first G1 win, arms raised, the relief visible. The former World TV Champion is on the board. '''Crowd:''' '''ONO''' chants — the building adopted the youngest man in the field. POP for the Ono Driver. Reyes applauded him on the way out.

* '''Match 3: Takagi def. Tanaka.''' The Dragon and the Bulldog — CHAOS's lariat against the Cannon Club's last enforcer standing. Tanaka, fighting for a faction that no longer exists, brawled like a man with nothing to lose: the guardrail, the ring post, the fourteen-minute war of attrition. Takagi's chest was red from Tanaka's chops by the halfway mark. The finish: Tanaka went for the Bulldog Bomb at 12; Takagi backdropped out, and the Dragon lariat ended it at 13:19. Takagi is 3-0; Tanaka falls to 1-2, and the Bulldog walked out slowly, alone, to the exit the Cannon Club once stormed through together. '''Crowd:''' Dueling '''TAKAGI''' / '''BULLDOG''' chants — the crowd respects the Bulldog's war. GASPS for the lariat.

* '''Match 4: Taumafa def. Diallo.''' The family warrior against the Hammer — the last Cannon Club loyalist in the tournament. Diallo, the Asikyran traditional wrestler, took Taumafa to the mat early and tried to grind him; Taumafa's power kept turning the mat game into a stand-up collision. The story of the match was the size of it — two of the biggest men in the field trading short-arm clotheslines that echoed through the gymnasium. The finish: Diallo went for the Hammer Slam at 11; Taumafa slipped behind, and the giant powerbomb at 12:08 made it three straight. Taumafa is 3-0, tied atop Block B — and the family's G1 is running hot while the chief's draws keep costing points. '''Crowd:''' '''TAUMAFA''' chants — the Aizawa crowd, Vaiahi diaspora to the core, sang for the family. ERUPTION for the powerbomb. Diallo got a respectful hand on the way out.

* '''Match 5: KAZUKI def. Jusuf.''' The main event — the shooting star against the truth-teller, the block's most anticipated stylistic collision. Jusuf's mosaraza — no selling, no bumping, no performing — met KAZUKI's aerial artistry, and the match was exactly the uncomfortable war the preview promised. Jusuf treated KAZUKI's early offense as beneath response; KAZUKI answered by going to the air — the springboard dropkick, the plancha, the missile dropkick, everything Jusuf could not ignore because it kept landing. The tension peaked at 14 minutes when Jusuf caught the mosaraza kneebar in the center of the ring — the hold that made Ishida tap in 8:56 — and KAZUKI, the man who survived four wars with the Tribal Chief, made the ropes. The finish: Jusuf went for the kneebar again at 17; KAZUKI rolled through, stacked him up — near fall — and the Twin Star Press at 17:56 ended it. Jusuf took his second loss — and this time he bumped. Clean pin. Jusuf walked out without acknowledging anyone. KAZUKI is 3-0, alone atop Block B. '''Crowd:''' '''KAZUKI''' chants dueling with uneasy silence for Jusuf — the crowd does not know what to do with him. GASPS at the kneebar. ERUPTION for the Twin Star Press. A standing ovation for both — the first time the Aizawa crowd stood for Jusuf Muntasir.

